  (item "archived-streams-leave-the-all-cards-view" :group "sprint" :area "nova-sprint"
   :title "Archived streams leave the all-cards view"
   :text "The dashboard's CARDS BY STATE strip and the all-cards view count only cards of streams on the board; archiving or removing a stream removes its landed cards from those views (today the strip still shows 28 landed from archived streams). Source: Glenn 2026-10-15 ~22:15Z: \"This is a bug for v1.5. When landed cards and/or work streams are removed, the landed cards they contained should be removed from the ALL CARDS view.\""
   :why "Kind: bug."
   :date "2026-10-15"
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "Glenn, 2026-10-15 ~22:15Z"
   :kept (:kind "bug"))
  (item "adopt-store-step-no-false-half-move" :group "ops" :area "nova-sprint"
   :title "The adopt step store does not report a false half move"
   :text "On every non-seat host the adopt prints \"REFUSED step=store, a half move\" even when the install worked. The step must report what it did. Found adopting v1.3.15 to v1.3.18 on 2026-10-15 (coordinator report)."
   :why "Kind: bug."
   :date "2026-10-15"
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "coordinator report, adopting v1.3.15 to v1.3.18, 2026-10-15"
   :kept (:kind "bug"))
  (item "adopt-friends-step-installs-freddy-with-width-and-model" :group "ops" :area "nova-sprint"
   :title "The adopt friends step installs freddy with width and model"
   :text "On mini-m5-1 the friends step refuses the freddy install because nova-friend install needs --width and --model, which the play does not pass. Found adopting v1.3.15 to v1.3.18 on 2026-10-15 (coordinator report)."
   :why "Kind: bug."
   :date "2026-10-15"
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "coordinator report, adopting v1.3.15 to v1.3.18, 2026-10-15"
   :kept (:kind "bug"))
  (item "adopt-window-stops-the-sprint-server-reliably" :group "ops" :area "nova-sprint"
   :title "The adopt window stops the sprint server reliably"
   :text "The studio adopt window does not always stop the sprint server within 180 s. The stop must drain readers and succeed, or say why. Found adopting v1.3.15 to v1.3.18 on 2026-10-15 (coordinator report)."
   :why "Kind: bug."
   :date "2026-10-15"
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "coordinator report, adopting v1.3.15 to v1.3.18, 2026-10-15"
   :kept (:kind "bug"))
  (item "adopt-warns-when-a-limit-host-is-not-in-the-inventory" :group "ops" :area "nova-sprint"
   :title "Adopt warns when a --limit host is not in the inventory"
   :text "adopt --limit <host> silently does nothing for a host missing from nova-inventory. It must warn, naming the host. Found adopting v1.3.15 to v1.3.18 on 2026-10-15 (coordinator report)."
   :why "Kind: bug."
   :date "2026-10-15"
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "coordinator report, adopting v1.3.15 to v1.3.18, 2026-10-15"
   :kept (:kind "bug"))
  (item "live-agrees-with-hook-status" :group "ops" :area "nova-sprint"
   :title "nova-sprint live agrees with nova-hook status"
   :text "nova-sprint live refused while nova-hook status showed proven (seen 2026-10-15 ~20:5xZ). Pull request 416 (v1.3.17, the hook gate accepts a silent proven name) may have fixed it: verified 2026-10-15 on v1.3.18, live reports OK while status is proven. Close unless it recurs; kept as a regression check. Found adopting v1.3.15 to v1.3.18 on 2026-10-15 (coordinator report)."
   :why "Kind: bug."
   :date "2026-10-15"
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "coordinator report, adopting v1.3.15 to v1.3.18, 2026-10-15"
   :kept (:kind "bug"))
  (item "a-friend-row-takes-its-ready-queue-by-itself" :group "friends" :area "nova-sprint"
   :title "A friend row takes its ready queue by itself"
   :text "A friend row whose seat is live takes every card dealt to its ready queue at once, up to its width, without any session deciding; the take starts the worker the row's harness configures, at a model no lower than the card's tier (heavy and frontier go to the row's strongest model). A card the row cannot run is refused back to the deal with a judgment naming why, never left sitting ready. Where shows the dealt-to-taken latency per row; a ready card untaken past 60 s on a live row with free width raises one judgment. The model (FriendReadyTake) gains the invariant that a live row with free width holds no ready card past one tick, with a reversed witness. Tests run on the in-memory twin with time as an argument. The full brief is kept at working/tmp/v15cards/a-friend-row-takes-its-ready-queue-by-itself.md. Source: Glenn 2026-10-16 ~00:35Z: \"Add a card to v1.5 to setup something so you take cards in ready queue automatically. that is the disgn and the intent.\""
   :why "Cards sat in a friend row's ready queue for long stretches on 2026-10-15 because taking waited for a dispatcher session to decide."
   :date "2026-10-16"
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "Glenn, 2026-10-16 ~00:35Z"
   :kept (:kind "feature"))
  (item "a-friends-dispatcher-follows-its-row-width-by-itself" :group "friends" :area "nova-sprint"
   :title "A friend's dispatcher follows its row width by itself"
   :text "The width in nova-config is the one true source for how many workers a friend's dispatcher runs. Today the coordinator changes `nova-config friend set <f> --width N` and then has to message the friend to resize her dispatcher child; that second step goes away. friend-sync already writes the width to the friend's row. Either the friend's hook pushes a width-changed notice, or the dispatcher reads the row width (from `nova-sprint friend cards <f>` or a `friend width` verb) on every loop and runs up to that many workers, with reads at half a slot. Nobody tells the friend. Tests: a unit test with no real time, where changing the configured width changes the dispatcher's concurrency on its next loop; and a functional test on a real store, where shrinking the width never kills a running worker and only stops new takes. The width/take loop is a state machine, so the TLA+ module is extended (FriendReadyTake) or a new one added: the width the dispatcher runs to equals the row width within one loop, and a shrink never terminates a running worker, each with a reversed witness. Related: the v1.5 entry a-friend-row-takes-its-ready-queue-by-itself (#431). Source: Glenn 2026-10-16: \"Each friend should *automatically* update their dispatcher when their width changes in nova-config. It should not be a second step.\""
   :why "Changing a friend's width needed a second step, a message to the friend, so the dispatcher could run at the old width until someone remembered."
   :date "2026-10-16"
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "Glenn, 2026-10-16"
   :kept (:kind "feature"))
  (item "friend-deal-rotation-index" :group "friends" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "The friend deal rotates over equally idle friends"
   :text "preferredFriend (internal/sprint/friend_deal.go) picks the friend with the most idle lanes, then the most free room, then the first by name, so a tie always goes to the same friend. A persisted friend_deal_index on the fleet table, kept like deal_index (round.go PropDealIndex), breaks the tie as ((index + 1) % n), so the first name never always wins. A unit test shows two equally idle friends alternate across ticks, and tla/WhoPreference.tla gains a fairness property: no eligible friend is starved while another is dealt two."
   :why "A new deal counter, held out of the 1.4.x point releases, which take only critical breakers."
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "Glenn, 2026-10-16: \"Remember to do the ( index + 1 ) % n when dealing so you don't bias to the top.\" (scoped out of v1.4.4 by the freeze rule)")
  (item "rebalance-loads-read-history" :group "sprint" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "fleet level --rebalance loads the read history it deals from"
   :text "FleetStep (internal/sprint/store/steps.go) loads only the fleet and work tables, with no Extras, so a rebalance from the fleet level verb deals a moved read (rebalance.go readCardIDFor) without the retired generations or the verdicts of the reader it picks. A spent reader can be offered the read again, and a generation that collides with a retired record is refused by the store. The tick's own rebalance loads tickExtras and is correct. The fix gives FleetStep the readers table and ReadCardExtras for its level and rebalance operations, with a test that fails on the old load."
   :why "Pre-existing, found by the cold read of #436, not tonight's wedge; a collision is refused, never lost."
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "the cold reader of nova-sprint #436, 2026-10-16")
  (item "friend-lanes-per-tier-model" :group "friends" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "A friend's card lanes run each card on the model of its tier"
   :text "A friend's one-shot lane runs every card on the friend's one model, whatever the card's tier. The friend row names a model per tier it serves, and a lane launches each card, work or read, on the model of the card's tier, never below it (the rule of #432, a card is never worked or read below its tier)."
   :why "A new row field and lane launch rule."
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "the coordinator, 2026-10-16")
  (item "machine-capability-tags" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "Machine capability tags for the deal (kvm, XDP)"
   :text "A card that needs a machine capability, such as kvm or a held XDP host, names it, and the deal gives it only to a member whose nova-config machine row carries the tag. The beat's gate facts (deal.go MemberFacts, v1.4.3) are the measured half; the tags are the declared half, checked against the facts so a tag with no measured fact is refused. space keeps its own rule: XDP functional tests only on a held XDP machine, never on a shared bench."
   :why "New configuration and deal matching."
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "the coordinator, 2026-10-16")
  (item "release-notes-identical-across-repos" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "Identical release notes across the lockstep repositories"
   :text "A lockstep release (nova-sprint and nova-tools at one version) publishes the same notes in both repositories, written once and checked by the release tool, not two hand-kept files that drift: tonight's nova-tools notes still say \"The matching stable tools release for nova sprint v1.4.0\" at v1.4.4."
   :why "Release tooling, not a fix to the sprint."
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "the coordinator, 2026-10-16")
  (item "no-progress-attempt-escalates-tier" :group "sprint" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "A no-progress attempt escalates the card's tier"
   :text "Overnight at least four cards (java-ledgers, java-tables-soak, elixir-porting-register, java-accessor) were redealt at pro after a HOLD read, and the new pro attempt left every file the reader named unchanged, or touched only comments; each was read and held again, up to four times. When a read HOLDs and the next attempt's diff touches none of the files and lines the previous read named, the next deal raises the card's tier one step (pro to heavy) and notes why. A unit test with no real time drives the hold, the no-progress attempt and the raised deal, and the TLA+ model of the attempts gains the escalation with a reversed witness that redeals at the same tier."
   :why "A new escalation rule in the deal."
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "Rowan, 2026-10-16, from the overnight fix column")
  (item "friend-row-git-identity" :group "friends" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "A friend's own git identity on her nova-config row"
   :text "friend stage sets the clone's identity from the friend row's git_identity, but the nova-config friend kind has no such field (the row's fields are tiers, roles, width, mode, config_dir, token_cap, streams, kinds, pronouns and session), so every friend delivery was refused until v1.4.5 fell back to the sprint row's git_identity. The friend kind gains git_identity (a migration, apply, and nova-config friend set --git_identity), each friend's identity set by the owner, and stage prefers it (identity=friend)."
   :why "A schema migration on the fleet's configuration store, held out of the point release."
   :release "v1.5"
   :origin "the coordinator's tool audit, 2026-10-16")
  (item "selftest-and-tests-run-with-routes-configured-as-in-use" :group "sprint" :area "nova-sprint" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "Selftest, the sims and the unit tests run with routes configured, as in use"
   :text "A store with no routes configured at all treats every tier as served, and each worker runs its own --model. v1.4.6 fences that shortcut so it applies only to the in-memory twin: the pretend sprint of `nova-sprint selftest`, the help walkthrough, and the twin and play sims. A real store with no routes refuses by name. v1.7 removes the shortcut entirely. The selftest twin, the walkthrough, the sims and the roughly 362 unit tests that rely on an empty route table instead configure routes the way a real sprint does, through the same nova-config route rows or their store equivalent, so the tier is respected everywhere with no special case. DONE-WHEN: the predicate that fences the shortcut is deleted; no code path serves a tier without an enabled route; selftest, the walkthrough and the sims configure routes; the full unit tier and TLC (ReaderTiers) pass; and a test pins that an empty route table refuses on every store kind."
   :why "The system should be tested exactly as it will be used. A special case that serves every tier when no routes exist is a path a real sprint never takes, so the tests that rely on it prove less than they appear to, and keeping it, even fenced, leaves a way for the tier rule to be skipped."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Glenn, 2026-10-16: \"Add a task to v1.7 to do it properly. It's best to test exactly as the system will be used.\" (the v1.4.6 fence of the empty-route shortcut)"
   :kept (:kind "refactor"))
  (item "swarm-mode-every-lane-on-one-problem" :group "sprint" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "Swarm mode: the whole fleet and every friend go wide on one problem"
   :text "A new mode beside the deal. Instead of many cards to many workers, one hard problem goes to every lane at once: every fleet member's lanes and every friend's width, each attacking the same problem independently. Coordination is shared across the message bus plus a few text files in a repo: a findings log (what each lane learned), a dead-ends list (what failed and why, so no lane repeats it), and a leaderboard of candidate heads with their gate results. The problem's tests, or an outside judge, rank the candidates; the first head that passes every gate wins, or the best one at a deadline. Design questions for the card: diversity across lanes (different models, tiers, prompts and starting points, so lanes are not copies); how much each lane reads of the shared files, and when (sharing too early collapses diversity, too late wastes work); a budget and a stop rule (a token cap, a wall clock, and stopping once a winner lands); merging partial results from several lanes; and fairness of the bus under many writers. It is a state machine (lanes, candidates, judging, stop), so its TLA+ model comes first. Predicated on nova-tools v1.7 rename-nova-swarm-to-nova-worker, which frees the name: this mode is nova-swarm."
   :why "Some problems are worth many independent attempts at once: hard bugs, R&D and frontier cards. Today the sprint can only split work, not converge many minds on one problem."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Glenn, live, 2026-10-16: 'for v1.7 we could add a swarm mode. This is where entire fleet and friends go wide with each lane solving the same problem, with shared coordination across the message bus and some text files in a repo.'")
  (item "composite-verbs-one-call-per-coordinator-act" :group "sprint" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "Composite verbs: one call does each coordinator act, end to end"
   :text "Learned from Hermes Agent's programmatic tool calling, which collapses a multi-step pipeline into one inference call. Each coordinator act that today takes 3 to 6 verbs, with traps on the way, becomes one deterministic verb that does the whole act and prints only what to act on. Measured on 2026-10-16: (1) stuck: list every card that is not moving but could be, and why: waiting with every need landed, held by a judgment, or merging but not queued. This is today's tools/stuck-cards-review.sh made a verb. (2) refinish-same-head: re-finish a card whose only fault was the bench or the gate, checking first that the head merges onto the base. (3) fix-missing-base: add a recut card's missing REPO or BASE line in any column (return, brief --keep-head, accept). (4) resolve-bound: clear a misclassified attempt bound (release, then an in-place brief with a dated carry line). (5) accept-on-read: record a cold read with its evidence file, choosing --heavy or --read-ok itself. Each verb has --dry-run, refuses by name with the one next command, and is tested on a real store, including every refusal. Glenn, 2026-10-16: skills are non-deterministic, so lessons become deterministic verbs and checks, never prose skills the agent must remember to apply."
   :why "Every manual multi-verb act today cost tokens and hit at least one refusal that needed another verb first; one deterministic verb per act removes both."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Glenn, live, 2026-10-16, on the Hermes Agent review: '#2 is great idea for v1.7' and 'skills are fine, but remember, skills are non-deterministic'")
  (item "lessons-become-deterministic-checks" :group "sprint" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "Every resolved judgment becomes a recorded lesson, and repeated lessons become verbs or checks"
   :text "Learned from Hermes Agent's skill loop (the agent writes a skill after solving something and improves it with use), but made deterministic. Glenn, 2026-10-16: skills are non-deterministic. The machine records every resolved judgment as data: the judgment kind, the cause the coordinator named, the verbs that fixed it, and how long the card was stuck. When a cause repeats past a threshold, the machine raises a named judgment proposing the fix as code: a composite verb (composite-verbs-one-call-per-coordinator-act), a refusal that names the next command, or a check that stops the cause at its source, so a hulk-style bench fault or a recut card with no BASE line is caught before it strands cards. A lesson never lives only as prose for an agent to remember. Today's examples, to seed it: a bench gate on the wrong Go, a misclassified attempt bound, a recut card missing REPO and BASE lines blocking a whole stream's batch, a false empty-commit on a no-ff merge over a carried head, soft land-after deps on file overlap, and stale GATE-NEEDS judgments after members gained facts."
   :why "The coordinator fixed the same few causes by hand many times today; recording them as data and turning repeats into code makes the fix permanent and deterministic."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Glenn, live, 2026-10-16, on the Hermes Agent review: '#2 and #3 is great for v1.7' and 'skills are fine, but remember, skills are non-deterministic'")
  (item "card-lint-locks-in-the-coordinator-lessons" :group "sprint" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "Card lint locks in the coordinator's lessons: 16 new rules from 30 hours of failed cards"
   :text "From the coaching analysis of 408 failed events, 2026-10-16 (~/rowan-working/coaching/2026-10-16.md, section 4). 78 events, plus 57 machine PATHS widenings and 156 brief-replaced bounces, were the brief's fault, not the worker's. Each lesson becomes a deterministic rule at add, brief, recut and --widen, refusing with the one next command: L1 generated-outputs-in-paths (needs a per-repo map of which emitter writes which generated files); L2 registry-rows-in-paths (extends paths-cover-registry to make/<lang>.mk, make/negative-controls.json, tablenames, tla CASES/RUNS/RUNS-HISTORY, tools/agentsmap/catalog.go); L3 pinned-digest-in-paths; L4 changed-rule-pins-in-paths (tests and docs that pin the rule being changed); L5 every-brief-names-repo-and-base; L6 needs-are-header-lines-and-live (a named dependency is a DEPENDS-ON or LAND-AFTER line naming a live card); L7 overlap-on-the-table-needs-an-order; L8 donewhen-runs-at-base (every target, script, driver, TLA module, TLC group and sibling exists at BASE or inside PATHS/NEW); L9 siblings-named; L10 host-words-need-gate-needs; L11 staged-job-or-machine-only; L12 roadmap-flip-needs-row-coverage; L13 measure-card-names-its-driver (and refuses a hand-made board); L14 form-has-a-checker (needs a review-checker command); L15 pin-only-to-an-up-friend; L16 tier-floor-by-size (only ever raises a tier). Also from today: BASE must be a stream branch, never another card's branch, since a card that landed onto a card branch never reached its stream (rocketnet-message-objects-and-c-api). Every rule is tested on a real store, including each refusal, and replayed against today's failed cards to show it would have caught them."
   :why "Lessons that live in prose are forgotten; skills are non-deterministic (Glenn, same day). A lint rule refuses the bad card before it costs an attempt."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Glenn, live, 2026-10-16: 'as coordinator, please coach yourself how you can improve the cards that go in, and then lock it in with card lint (v1.7)'")
  (item "one-startup-supervisor-configured-by-friends" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "One app starts at login and runs every nova service on the machine, configured from the friend list, never a launch agent per friend or job"
   :text "After the 2026-10-16 reboot the studio had 45 files in ~/Library/LaunchAgents and more in /Library/LaunchDaemons, a plist for each friend hook (aurora, emma, johnny, stella), each friend runner, each loop, the dashboards, the redis instances and the sprint server. 19 were stale: editor backups, before-* and moved-to-* copies, a duplicate Stella hook, and sprint-table-live, which had crash-looped 23,923 times on a retired verb. Two more broke on the reboot because their paths pointed at a versioned Homebrew Cellar node and at /tmp. Each friend added, moved or retired meant a hand-written plist, and nothing removed the old one. The fix is one supervisor, installed as the only nova launch agent on a machine (plus one daemon only where root is needed, such as the CI runners). It reads which friends live on this machine, their harness, dirs and sessions from nova-config, and the machine's roles (store, sprint server, dashboard, hook-serve, loops) from the same config. It starts each child, restarts it with backoff, writes one log per child, and reports per-child state so nova-doctor shows a crash loop at once. Adding or moving a friend is a config change: the supervisor starts or stops her children, and a retired job disappears because config is the only source. It refuses a path under /tmp or a versioned Cellar path, and nova-doctor flags any other nova plist on the machine as a stray. The supervisor's states (absent, starting, running, backing-off, stopped, removed) get a TLA+ model before code, and unit tests use a fake clock and a fake process starter. Covers nova-tools binaries too (nova-hook, nova-friend, nova-swarm loops, nova-secrets exec wrappers), and the fleet's ansible plays install only the supervisor."
   :why "A launch agent per friend and per job grew to dozens of plists that nobody removed, and stale ones broke things and loaded the machine (Glenn, 2026-10-16)."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Glenn, live, 2026-10-16: 'consolidate all these things into one app that starts at startup and has configuration according to friends, not an app per-friend'")
  (item "survey-log-caps-its-output" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "nova-sprint log honours --max and names the rest"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint log (cmd/nova-sprint/log.go), never a second mechanism. Problem: `nova-sprint log --since 1h --max 5` printed 2,985 entries, ending `LOG OK lines=2985 of=127415` (854 KB). log.go never reads c.max, and log.go:120 prints every line. Smallest fix: honour --max with a default of 20, end a cut list with `MORE kind=log shown=<n> total=<t> run: nova-sprint log <same filters> --max 0`, and add --grep and --kind later if needed. The help text is corrected by the v1.4.x text fix (the store-verbs --max paragraph now says log does not cap yet); this is the behaviour, and the fix reverses that paragraph when it lands. Tool: nova-sprint log. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A bounded look at the log cost 854 KB because --max is parsed and ignored."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 1 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-check-baseline-and-rule-table" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "nova-sprint check separates new faults from old ones and says what each rule is"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint check (rules in internal/sprint/log.go). Problem: the live sprint prints `CHECK FAILED violations=241`, and every line reads `VIOLATION rule 13: fleet/<card> is not on the table and the log last moved it to <m>:ok`. No help, playbook or repair text names rule 13; in the code it is log/table agreement (log.go:200-235: a card's place and generation on the table must match the log's last move). Smallest fix: (a) a rule table in `help check`, one line per rule with its remedy, which needs someone who knows each remedy (it is not confirmed that `repair` clears rule 13); (b) check --baseline or --since <epoch> to accept old violations so new ones show. The MORE line of check printing `...` is corrected by the v1.4.x text fix; this is the behaviour. Tool: nova-sprint check. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A check that is always red shows nobody a new fault, and rule 13 has no explanation anywhere."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 3 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-judgment-alias-documented" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "say what a judgment alias is, and print it in the answer commands"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint inbox, ack and wait. Problem: every judgment prints `alias=j20532`, and no help or playbook says what it is or which verbs accept it. `--answers` and `hold --cause` take j<n>; `ack` takes <note> only (not confirmed whether ack accepts aliases). Smallest fix: one words entry (`alias  a judgment's short name, j<n>; taken by hold --cause, answer --answers, ...`), then print the alias in the ack and wait lines wherever the verb accepts it. That alone would cut the inbox by more than half. Tool: nova-sprint inbox/ack/wait. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "Commands print 60-character ids when a five-character alias is accepted, and nothing says so."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 7 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-one-command-per-judgment" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "one recommended command per judgment, never a contradiction"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint inbox (internal/sprint/coordinator_pass.go:289, drift.go:130/151 `decisions: []string{\"act\", \"wait\"}`, printed by reads.go:2793 like any decision word). Problem: \"The seat is not answering\" says `this cannot be waited or acknowledged` and is followed by `act:` then `nova-sprint wait tick-overdue-...~21-1.3 --for 30m`; the presence judgment's `act:` offers both ack and wait; one `act:` block was empty. Which judgment owned the wait line was not settled from code alone. Smallest fix: drop a decision block that repeats a listed decision, never print `wait` under a judgment whose type WaitRefused refuses, and print one recommended line. This changes output a parser reads, hence v1.7. Tool: nova-sprint inbox. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "The inbox tells the coordinator to wait on a judgment that refuses a wait."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 8 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-usage-lines-list-playbook-flags" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "every flag a playbook uses is in its verb's usage line, held by a test"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Sweep over the verb table (cmd/nova-sprint/verbs.go) and nova-hook hook (nova-tools cmd/nova-hook/main.go:235), never a second list. Problem: the sprint playbook uses `rework --carry`, `brief --replace-in-flight`/`--keep-head`, `drop --cascade|--relink|--waive`, `--who/--state/--ids-file`, `hold --cause`, `set --pegged-bound`, `fleet up --deadline|--harnesses`, `ack --dry-run` and `friend take --all-or-nothing`, and none is in its verb's usage line. The playbook test reads flags from the FlagSet and the synopsis together (playbook_test.go:47-48), so the gap passes. nova-hook hook documents --codex-socket/--codex-thread/--codex-events-dir/--codex-since in its detail but not in its usage; that usage is quoted in nova-tools TOOLS-PLAYBOOK.md:52 and, across repos, in COORDINATOR-PLAYBOOK.md:58. About a dozen usage lines, each with its quotes kept in step. Smallest fix: a test that every flag a playbook passes appears in the usage synopsis, then add them. The brief-edit warning (survey-brief-edit-in-flight-warning) belongs in the same sweep. Tools: nova-sprint (many verbs), nova-hook hook. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A cold AI reading the usage line cannot find flags the playbook tells it to pass."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 10 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-retire-one-release-aliases" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "retire the one-release aliases on a named release, and the disabled lane tutorial"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Across nova-sprint, nova-config, nova-redis and nova-check. Problem: aliases are 'kept for one release' with no release named. nova-sprint: `--limit` (verbs.go:653, \"accepted for one release\"); `fleet down`, `reader away`, `friend down`, `reader up` and `friend up` (hold.go:38-41, 227-235); `fleet hold`/`friend hold` beside `hold`. nova-config `--as` and `--friend` (main.go:684, login.go:404). nova-redis `--addr` (main.go:318). nova-check `--fail-max` (per the survey, not checked). Also the three `lane` usage lines and the lane tutorial (cmd/nova-sprint/lane.go), DISABLED since 2026-10-13; the \"until v1.3\" date is corrected by the v1.4.x text fix, and removing the tutorial is this item. Glenn names the release; then delete each alias, its usage lines and its NOTE, with the tests that pin them (cap_alias_test.go). Tools: nova-sprint, nova-config, nova-redis, nova-check. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "Removing an alias changes behaviour, and 'accepted until vX' is Glenn's decision."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 11, 12 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-seat-login-in-help" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "list seat login and seat logout in the help"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends the verb table (cmd/nova-sprint/verbs.go) and the playbook test. `nova-sprint seat login` exists (dispatched inside seat, seat.go:505-509, storelogin.go:193; docs/CLI.md:172-173, :386), so \"the address nova-sprint seat login recorded\" (main.go:698) is correct. The gap: `seat login` and `seat logout` appear in no usage line of `nova-sprint help` or `help seat`, so a cold AI cannot find the verb the --redis text names. Smallest fix: add them to the verb table or the seat synopsis, and to the playbook test. Tool: nova-sprint seat. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "The --redis flag text names a verb no usage line lists; the dispatch and table change, so it is not a text fix."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 16 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-one-doctor" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "one doctor: fold nova-swarm doctor into nova-doctor and retire nova-sprint doctor"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Spans nova-sprint and nova-tools. Problem: `nova-doctor run`, `nova-sprint doctor [--all] [--area ...] [--preflight ...]` (in help and step 5 of the playbook's session start) and `nova-swarm doctor` (cmd/nova-swarm/main.go:51, \"refuse a launch under a shadowed nova-swarm\"). The coordinator's own rule is 'only nova-doctor; nova-sprint doctor retired by name'. Smallest fix (Glenn decides): fold nova-swarm doctor into nova-doctor as `--check swarm-shadow`, and retire nova-sprint doctor from help and the playbook, or rename it to what it is. A text-only line on nova-swarm (\"one check; nova-doctor run is the machine's doctor\") is not worth a separate PR before that decision. Tools: nova-sprint doctor, nova-swarm doctor. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "Three tools are called doctor, and the rule says there is one."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 17 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-one-refusal-shape" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "one refusal shape, <TOOL> <VERB> REFUSED: <cause>; run: <command>, on stderr, with a not-found remedy"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Lint over every refuse/emit call in nova-sprint and nova-tools. Examples of other shapes: `nova-sprint inbox REFUSED: unknown flag ...` (exit 2), `DOCTOR REFUSED: ...`, and `SPILL NOTE ...` beside the result. Most tools already share the shape. Smallest fix: a lint that rejects any other shape, plus the not-found remedy `run: nova-sprint view cards --stream <s>` in place of the biggest view, `where`. The REFUSED word in the `card` and `priority` not-found lines is corrected by the v1.4.x text fix; this is the lint and the remedy. Tools: all. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "Callers parse refusals, and a few still print a different shape."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 19 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-help-grouped-index" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "nova-sprint help is a 5 KB grouped index, and the prose moves to topics"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint help (cmd/nova-sprint). Problem: the banner is 749 lines and 60,368 bytes with about 181 usage lines and no one-line purpose for each; the judgment table, the fleet beat rules, the friend report grammar and the land algorithm are all in it. Smallest fix: group the verbs (Seat, Cards, Judgments, Fleet, Friends, Readers, Streams, Landing, Release, Machine, Admin), one line per verb with a purpose, and move today's text to `help all` and topics (`help judgments`, `help land`, `help fleet`). The interim line \"The whole usage and prose follow (60 KB)...\" goes with it. Tool: nova-sprint help. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A cold AI pays 60 KB before it finds a verb."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 20 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-short-common-flags-help-store" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "short common-flag lines in help <verb>, and one help store topic"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends help <verb> (cmd/nova-sprint). Problem: `help ack` is 1,603 bytes, of which 942 are the common flags `--actor/--epoch/--json/--max/--op/--redis`, and --redis alone is 330 characters (main.go:698). Smallest fix: one-line descriptions of the six, plus a new `help store` topic holding the full --redis paragraph. A new topic is new behaviour, and many -h outputs change, hence v1.7. The V14X store-verbs paragraph edit does not overlap. Tool: nova-sprint. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "Boilerplate flags take 60% of each verb's help."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 21 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-inbox-brief" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "inbox --brief: one line per group, with inbox --open <alias> for the full commands"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint inbox. Problem: 38 judgments came to 73 KB in one run, and about 15 KB in another. Smallest fix: one line per group (`j20532 a reader found it broken stream=schema-dart size=3 waited=39m OVERDUE`), with `inbox --open <alias>` for the full commands. Also document `safe_default` (from SafeDecisionDefault) in `help inbox`, after checking what each value means; the missing `[--json]` in the usage line is corrected by the v1.4.x text fix. Tool: nova-sprint inbox. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "The inbox is too large to read in one turn."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 22, 35 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-inbox-open-prints-its-group" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "inbox --open prints only the group it opens"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint inbox (verbs.go:2440-2460). Problem: `inbox --open tick-presence-...~21-1.10` printed every judgment (about 15 KB), then that group's members under it, while `help inbox` says `list every member, need and notification of the group of this id`. Smallest fix: print the opened group alone. Tool: nova-sprint inbox. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "The verb named open prints the whole inbox."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 23 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-overlapping-verbs-table" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "one table for return, redo, rework, continue, brief, recut and the tier verbs"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends docs/COORDINATOR-PLAYBOOK.md section 4. Problem: from help alone a cold AI could not tell what `tier <card> --set` adds over `brief --tier`. Smallest fix: a side-by-side table (keeps id, keeps head, spends an attempt, when to use), with each verb's semantics checked against the code before it is written, which is why it is not a quick text fix. Tool: nova-sprint docs. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "The overlapping verbs have no side-by-side table, and the semantics must be checked in code first."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 24 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-brief-edit-in-flight-warning" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "the 'brief is wrong' row warns that a PATHS edit on a working card bounces its next finish"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends docs/COORDINATOR-PLAYBOOK.md. The 'the brief is wrong' row does not warn that a PATHS edit on a working card makes its next finish bounce unless `--replace-in-flight` or `--keep-head` is used. Add the warning once survey-usage-lines-list-playbook-flags settles the flags and the semantics are checked. Tool: nova-sprint docs. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "It waits on the usage-line sweep, so the warning names flags that are in the usage lines."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 25 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-no-circular-stop-and-look" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "no circular remedy: card's now: block drops its own stop-and-look line, and HELD reads plainly"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint card (RepeatDecision, internal/sprint/notes.go:183; held.go:24-29, :297-314). Problem: a judgment's `stop and look:` line says `nova-sprint card <id>`, and `card <id>` prints, in its `now:` block, the same `stop and look: nova-sprint card <id>`. Smallest fix: in card's now:, drop the stop-and-look line for the card being shown; an output change on a parsed block. Also the HELD line: print `writes the judgment <type>` in place of `writes <type>`, which changes `card --fields` that parsers read. The HELD holder letters (a) to (e) were documented in the words entry in v1.4.x; this is the output. Tool: nova-sprint card. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A remedy that points at itself leaves the coordinator where it started."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 27, 28 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-card-compact-default-view" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "card's default view is the header, now:, the attempts and the first 15 lines of the brief"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint card. Problem: each look at a card costs 2-3k tokens because briefs run 7-9 KB. Smallest fix: make the full brief opt-in with --brief, which already exists in the usage (verbs.go:168). Tool: nova-sprint card. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A routine look at a card should not cost a brief."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 29 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-judgment-human-titles" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "a human title per judgment type, printed beside the type"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint inbox. Problem: the presence judgment's title is the bare type `status`, with the body \"fleet member amd-2 is flapping\". The type is a parsed field, so retitling it is not a text fix. Smallest fix: a human title per type (`a member is flapping`) printed beside the type. Tool: nova-sprint inbox. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "The title 'status' says nothing about what happened."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 30 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-log-no-empty-payloads" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "log lines never end at an empty payload"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint log. Problem: `judgment updated: decision escalation delivery: ` and `ctl-schema-js: schema-js changed by rowan: ` have nothing after the colon. Smallest fix: print the changed field, or skip the line. Tool: nova-sprint log. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A log line with nothing after the colon records nothing."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 31 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-one-more-line-shape" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "one MORE line shape, held by a lint"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends the listed function (verbs.go:1139), needs.go:396 and the inbox. Problem: `MORE kind=... run: ...` (verbs.go:1139), needs.go:396 adds `omitted= first_hidden=`, and the inbox prints `... all: nova-sprint inbox --open <id>`. Smallest fix: one shape, `MORE kind=<k> shown=<n> total=<t> run: <exact command>`, held by a lint. The `...` in the listed MORE line is corrected by the v1.4.x text fix; this is the shape. Tool: nova-sprint. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A parser must handle three MORE shapes."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 33 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-friend-status-view" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "friend cards <f> (or friend show <f>) heads with status, hold reason, hook state and last beat"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. Extends nova-sprint friend. Problem: `friend cards rowan` shows 8 working cards and no status, so there is no single 'why is this friend here' view; the playbook's `nova-friend check/reach` need the bus address that nova-hook refuses to find (see survey-bus-tools-read-seat-login). Smallest fix: head the view with status, hold reason, hook state and last beat. Tool: nova-sprint friend. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "Answering why a friend is idle takes several verbs."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 34 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-work-card-stage-banner" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "decide the pre-alpha banner of nova-work and nova-card"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint. cmd/nova-work/main.go:49 and cmd/nova-card/main.go:33 print `... is pre-alpha: not ready for production use.` Both playbooks make `nova-work roadmap add` the standard way to record missing behaviour. Whether to drop the banner is Glenn's call, a stage decision rather than a text fix. Tools: nova-work, nova-card. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A stage decision is Glenn's, and the banner contradicts the playbooks until he makes it."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 57 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-bare-call-names-default-verb" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "a bare call to a tool with a default verb names that verb"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-tools. Extends pkg/tool (tool.go:171-173). Problem: for any tool with Default set (nova-doctor, nova-up) a bare call prints `no verb and no file given; the verbs are run, version; run: nova-doctor help`, and 'no file' means nothing to either tool. Smallest fix: when Default is set, say `no verb given; run: <tool> <Default>` (or --dry-run), or run the default verb when called bare, the better end. The `[run]` usage line is corrected by the v1.4.x text fix; this is the behaviour. Shared code across every tool. Tool: pkg/tool (nova-doctor, nova-up). Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "Shared code across every tool, so it is not a text fix."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 37 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-banner-json-sentence" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "the banner's --json sentence is true for tools whose verbs print their own lines"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-tools. Extends the banner generator (pkg/tool/tool.go:527-539), which writes \"Every verb but <verbs with f.Prints()> takes --json: <NoJSON>\". Problem: nova-doctor says `Every verb but run takes --json: run prints one DOCTOR ... line per check; --json prints results as one object` (doctor.go:239), and nova-check says the same of dogfood ledger/record/convergence/spelling and then \"spelling and convergence take it too\" (cmd/nova-check/main.go:54). Smallest fix: in the generator, list only verbs with no --json flag, and rewrite the two NoJSON strings. Tools: pkg/tool, nova-doctor, nova-check. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "The banner contradicts itself in two tools, and the generator is shared."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 38 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-doctor-friend-row-evidence" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "doctor friend-row names the command it ran and its refusal, and skips non-friend daemons"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-tools. Extends nova-doctor (internal/doctor/check_friend_row.go:26-28), which runs `nova-config friend show <d.Name> --json`. Problem: the live output names the daemon `sync`, which is no friend, probably the `nova-sprint friend sync` unit misread as a friend daemon by friendDaemonsOrOK, and the fix line (`friend set <name> --mode one-shot ...`) is wrong for it. Smallest fix: exclude non-friend daemons, and quote the exact argv and stderr of the failed call. Tool: nova-doctor. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A check that blames a non-friend and prescribes a wrong fix sends the operator the wrong way."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 39 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-doctor-gosdk-warn" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "nova-doctor gosdk warns outside a checkout"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-tools. Extends nova-doctor (check_gosdk.go:146). Problem: `DOCTOR gosdk fail no go.mod here: the bench's toolchain is not named`, so every run from /tmp exits 2. Smallest fix: warn, with `fix: pass --dir <checkout>`. That changes the status and the exit code. Tool: nova-doctor. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A run from any directory but a checkout exits 2 for a reason that is not a fault."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 40 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-store-write-effect" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "a StoreWrite effect in pkg/tool, used by every store-writing verb"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-tools. Extends pkg/tool (tool.go:139-141, Inspection, LocalWrite, Delivery) and nova-redis. Problem: `nova-redis spill -h` says `Effect: tool.LocalWrite` (main.go:157, 'writes files on this machine'), but spill writes one Redis key, and Delivery ('sends beyond this machine') is not right for a loopback Redis either. Smallest fix: a StoreWrite effect (`store write: writes the store at --redis`). Tools: pkg/tool, nova-redis. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "No effect word is true for a store write."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 46 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-nova-up-modes" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "nova-up names its modes, not a hidden verb"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-tools. Extends nova-up (internal/up/tool.go:36-40). `up` is a real verb and the Default, so `nova-up up -h` is valid. The confusion is three spellings of one entry point: `nova-up --local`, `nova-up up`, and the refusal `the verbs are up, version`. Smallest fix: settle it together with survey-bare-call-names-default-verb (`UP REFUSED: no mode given; the modes are --local and --seat; run: nova-up --local --dry-run`). Tool: nova-up. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "It waits on the shared bare-call refusal."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 49 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-bus-tools-read-seat-login" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "one store resolution: nova-hook, nova-bus and nova-friend read the seat login"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-tools. Extends pkg/bus (open.go:97). Problem: in a shell where `nova-sprint check` reaches the store, `nova-hook status --as rowan` gives `STATUS REFUSED: --redis is required: NOVA_BUS_REDIS is unset, and with no NOVA_SPRINT_REDIS the fleet's bus row (...) cannot be read either; refusing to guess; run: nova-hook help`. nova-sprint resolves its store through the seat login (main.go:698); the bus tools do not read it. Smallest fix: pkg/bus reads the seat login's bus address the way nova-sprint does. Until then the refusal could say the seat login is not read by this tool; hold that text with the fix so the refusal is not changed twice. Tools: nova-hook, nova-bus, nova-friend. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "Two store resolutions answer differently in the same shell."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 55 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-environment-table" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "one environment table in the tools playbook, held by a test"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-tools. Extends docs/TOOLS-PLAYBOOK.md. Problem: 17 or more variables (`NOVA_SPRINT_REDIS`, `NOVA_REDIS_ADDR`, `NOVA_BUS_REDIS`, `NOVA_SPRINT_SERVER`, `NOVA_*_PASSWORD_ENV`, `NOVA_PG_DSN`, `NOVA_SEAT`, `NOVA_SPRINT_ACTOR`, `NOVA_SECRETS_*`, ...), and `--redis` means the sprint store in nova-sprint and nova-table and the bus store in nova-hook and nova-bus. Smallest fix: a section `## 0. The environment` (name, tools that read it, what it names, the fallback when unset), generated from the flag defaults or held by a test so it cannot drift. A new doc mechanism. Tools: all. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "A new doc mechanism, and the table must not drift."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 56 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-doctor-summary-line" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "nova-doctor run ends with a typed summary line"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-tools. Extends nova-doctor run. Problem: it prints 13 `DOCTOR <check> ...` lines and then nothing (exit 2). Smallest fix: end with `DOCTOR OK|FAILED ok=<n> warn=<n> fail=<n>`, as every other tool ends with a typed summary. New output. Tool: nova-doctor. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "Every other tool ends with a typed summary line."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 60 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "survey-nova-loop-help-version" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "every nova-* on PATH answers help and version, or leaves PATH"
   :text "Neither nova-sprint nor nova-tools holds the scripts (probably rowan-tools), so the repository is to be settled when the card is cut. Problem: `nova-loop help` gives `nova-loop: no command for help` (exit 2), and `nova-push-credential --help` gives `usage: nova-push-credential get | probe <url>`. nova-doctor's self check also lists `nova-hook-750b0842b`, `nova-sprint-startfix`, `nova-*-mirror.git` and others, and its fix line `nova-update apply --file <manifest> <name>` cannot be run for a stray file; the fix line is corrected by the v1.4.x text fix, and this is the exclusion. Smallest fix: add help and version cases to the two scripts (or move them off PATH); make the self check skip `*.git` directories and stray copies; clean the stray copies from the bin directory (fleet-admin housekeeping). Also one help form in every tool: the tools playbook's claim that `<verb> -h` is always safe is corrected by the v1.4.x text fix for nova-fuse; nova-loop has no help form, and nova-secrets lists only a bare `help`. Tools: nova-loop, nova-push-credential, nova-doctor self. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "The tools playbook promises one help form, and these tools do not answer it."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Survey of the nova tools by rowan-opus and rowan-fable, 2026-10-16, triage item 44, 43, 54 (verified against nova-sprint main a9670be4 and nova-tools main c4ce3886a)")
  (item "ci-pause-sprint-record" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "CI pause, part 1 of 3 (nova-sprint): fleet quiet --ci pauses a machine's runners for a bounded window, with the model"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-sprint, tier heavy. Extends fleet quiet (cmd/nova-sprint/fleetquiet.go; rule internal/sprint/fleet_quiet.go: Quiet, quietPlan, quietEnds, QuietLines), never a second mechanism: Quiet gains ci, FleetReq (internal/sprint/steps_work.go) carries it, the queue answer gains ci_pause (cmd/nova-sprint/view.go), fleet beat gains --runners (cmd/nova-sprint/fleet.go, stored in internal/sprint/presence.go), where gains the ci cell (cmd/nova-sprint/reads.go); docs/SPEC-SPRINT.md section fleet-quiet-machine-b.w7 and docs/CLI.md follow. THE MODEL, written first: tla/RunnerPause.tla (clock, pause, per runner state, job, interrupted; actions Tick, SetPause with until <= clock + Cap, EndPause, JobStart, JobEnd, GateStep, and RaceAssign, the accepted race of a job assigned between the gate's read and its SIGINT). Properties NeverSignalBusy, BoundedPause, PausedOnlyWhilePaused and the liveness ResumesAfterDeadline. Three reversed witnesses: MCRunnerPauseBrokenKillBusy fails NeverSignalBusy, MCRunnerPauseBrokenNoExpiry fails ResumesAfterDeadline, MCRunnerPauseBrokenNoCap fails BoundedPause; group runnerpause in tla/CASES.tsv, TLC on a Linux bench, records merged with tlacheck merge --keep. Tests: internal/sprint TestFleetQuietCIPausesAndEndsAtItsTime, cmd/nova-sprint TestFleetQuietCIVerbQueueBeatAndWhere. THE CONTRACT, shared by the three CI-pause entries (ci-pause-sprint-record, ci-pause-runner-gate, ci-pause-member-carrier): (1) Record: fleet quiet <m> (--for|--until) --reason <text> --ci sets the member's quiet with ci true; the deal effect is the quiet's as today; a CI quiet more than 24h from now is refused, nothing written, naming the cap (CIQuietMost); --end and the tick's quietEnds end the CI pause with the quiet; the QUIET line of a CI quiet ends '; CI paused'. (2) Carrier: queue --as <m> --json carries ci_pause {until RFC3339 UTC, until_unix, reason} while the asking member's CI quiet is active, and no ci_pause key otherwise. (3) Pause file: the member writes it atomically (temp file in the same directory, then rename) as {until, until_unix, set_unix, reason} while ci_pause is present and removes it when absent; its path is the runner unit's NOVA_CI_PAUSE_FILE: linux the seat's $HOME/nova-bench/runners/ci-pause.json, darwin /Users/Shared/nova-runners/ci-pause.json (mode 2775, group staff: the darwin runner seat is a separate account). (4) Gate, per runner, FAIL OPEN: a pause is active iff the file parses, now < until_unix and until_unix <= set_unix + 86400; absent, unreadable, past or over the cap is no pause, CI on, so a dead sprint or member can never leave CI off. States: enabled -> draining (pause active, the runner's Runner.Worker is running a job) -> paused (child stopped by SIGINT) -> enabled (pause not active: child started again); enabled goes straight to paused when idle; draining goes back to enabled with no signal when the pause lifts first. DRAIN, NEVER KILL: the gate never signals a child whose Runner.Worker is running. It polls every NOVA_GATE_EVERY seconds (default 15). (5) Gate report: <pause dir>/state/<runner name>.json {state, at_unix} on each change. (6) Beat and view: the member sends fleet beat <m> --runners enabled=<n>,draining=<n>,paused=<n>,unmanaged=<n> (unmanaged: a Runner.Listener with no state file, e.g. one started from a shell outside its unit) only when the installed fleet beat -h lists --runners; the beat stores the counts beside no_room and refuses a malformed word naming the form; where and where --all show one cell per fleet member: ci=on, ci=paused until <time>, ci=draining <n>, ci=unmanaged <n> (the remedy is a converge of that host's runners), ci=- with no counts; where --json carries the same. Unit tests use no real time (clocks start at 1000 seconds), no sockets, no network: fakes for the store, the server, the process table and the files; real runner control is functional-tier only, and converging the fleet stays the coordinator's act."
   :why "fleet quiet and fleet hold stop sprint deals only; nothing pauses a machine's GitHub Actions runners, and a quiet window on amd-1 on 2026-10-16 was made by stopping its runners by hand. A pause must let running jobs finish, can never stay on past its deadline, and shows in the sprint's views."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Glenn, live, 2026-10-16: 'Please add a card so you can disable CI from running on machines as needed. 1.7'")
  (item "ci-pause-runner-gate" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "CI pause, part 2 of 3 (rowan-tools fleet): every declared runner unit runs under a gate that drains on a pause and fails open at its deadline"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/rowan-tools, tier pro. Extends the runners play (fleet/runners.yml from fleet/runners.tsv) and its templates fleet/templates/runner.service.j2 (systemd user unit, ExecStart run.sh, Restart=always, KillSignal=SIGINT) and fleet/templates/runner.plist.j2 (launchd, runsvc.sh, KeepAlive), never a second mechanism: a new bash script fleet/files/nova-runner-gate <runner dir> <runner script> becomes ExecStart and ProgramArguments, runs the runner's own script as its child, implements (4) and (5), finds a job by a Runner.Worker whose command line names <runner dir>/bin/Runner.Worker, re-reads the table just before any signal, and forwards SIGTERM and SIGINT so a unit stop behaves as today. The play installs the gate, creates the pause and state directories and renders NOVA_CI_PAUSE_FILE. Test seams NOVA_GATE_NOW, NOVA_GATE_ONCE and NOVA_GATE_PS; tests fleet TestRunnerGateStates (every transition, the four fail-open cases, a busy runner never signalled) and TestRunnerUnitsRunTheGate; make -C fleet runners-check only, never a real converge. THE CONTRACT, shared by the three CI-pause entries (ci-pause-sprint-record, ci-pause-runner-gate, ci-pause-member-carrier): (1) Record: fleet quiet <m> (--for|--until) --reason <text> --ci sets the member's quiet with ci true; the deal effect is the quiet's as today; a CI quiet more than 24h from now is refused, nothing written, naming the cap (CIQuietMost); --end and the tick's quietEnds end the CI pause with the quiet; the QUIET line of a CI quiet ends '; CI paused'. (2) Carrier: queue --as <m> --json carries ci_pause {until RFC3339 UTC, until_unix, reason} while the asking member's CI quiet is active, and no ci_pause key otherwise. (3) Pause file: the member writes it atomically (temp file in the same directory, then rename) as {until, until_unix, set_unix, reason} while ci_pause is present and removes it when absent; its path is the runner unit's NOVA_CI_PAUSE_FILE: linux the seat's $HOME/nova-bench/runners/ci-pause.json, darwin /Users/Shared/nova-runners/ci-pause.json (mode 2775, group staff: the darwin runner seat is a separate account). (4) Gate, per runner, FAIL OPEN: a pause is active iff the file parses, now < until_unix and until_unix <= set_unix + 86400; absent, unreadable, past or over the cap is no pause, CI on, so a dead sprint or member can never leave CI off. States: enabled -> draining (pause active, the runner's Runner.Worker is running a job) -> paused (child stopped by SIGINT) -> enabled (pause not active: child started again); enabled goes straight to paused when idle; draining goes back to enabled with no signal when the pause lifts first. DRAIN, NEVER KILL: the gate never signals a child whose Runner.Worker is running. It polls every NOVA_GATE_EVERY seconds (default 15). (5) Gate report: <pause dir>/state/<runner name>.json {state, at_unix} on each change. (6) Beat and view: the member sends fleet beat <m> --runners enabled=<n>,draining=<n>,paused=<n>,unmanaged=<n> (unmanaged: a Runner.Listener with no state file, e.g. one started from a shell outside its unit) only when the installed fleet beat -h lists --runners; the beat stores the counts beside no_room and refuses a malformed word naming the form; where and where --all show one cell per fleet member: ci=on, ci=paused until <time>, ci=draining <n>, ci=unmanaged <n> (the remedy is a converge of that host's runners), ci=- with no counts; where --json carries the same. Unit tests use no real time (clocks start at 1000 seconds), no sockets, no network: fakes for the store, the server, the process table and the files; real runner control is functional-tier only, and converging the fleet stays the coordinator's act."
   :why "fleet quiet and fleet hold stop sprint deals only; nothing pauses a machine's GitHub Actions runners, and a quiet window on amd-1 on 2026-10-16 was made by stopping its runners by hand. A pause must let running jobs finish, can never stay on past its deadline, and shows in the sprint's views."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Glenn, live, 2026-10-16: 'Please add a card so you can disable CI from running on machines as needed. 1.7'")
  (item "ci-pause-member-carrier" :group "ops" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "CI pause, part 3 of 3 (nova-tools): the member writes the pause file from its queue answer and reports its runners on the beat"
   :text "Repository mas-bandwidth/nova-tools, tier pro. Extends nova-swarm member (pkg/member/member.go: Member.Beat, queueOut, noRoomArgs and drainArgs; cmd/nova-swarm/member.go: probeLoadUnmeasured, the pattern for probing --runners), never a second mechanism: new pkg/member/cipause.go keeps the pause file matching ci_pause per (3) (set_unix kept while until_unix is unchanged, nothing written when it already matches, a failed write one NOTE and a retry), reads the state directory and the process table (a seam) for the counts of (6), and Beat appends --runners only when the probe lists it; cmd/nova-swarm/member.go adds --ci-pause-file (default by OS per (3)); docs/CLI.md and docs/SPEC-SWARM.md follow. It lands safely before the other two parts: with no ci_pause key and no --runners in the help it writes and sends nothing extra. Tests: pkg/member TestCIPauseFileFollowsTheQueue, TestCIPauseBeatReportsRunners. THE CONTRACT, shared by the three CI-pause entries (ci-pause-sprint-record, ci-pause-runner-gate, ci-pause-member-carrier): (1) Record: fleet quiet <m> (--for|--until) --reason <text> --ci sets the member's quiet with ci true; the deal effect is the quiet's as today; a CI quiet more than 24h from now is refused, nothing written, naming the cap (CIQuietMost); --end and the tick's quietEnds end the CI pause with the quiet; the QUIET line of a CI quiet ends '; CI paused'. (2) Carrier: queue --as <m> --json carries ci_pause {until RFC3339 UTC, until_unix, reason} while the asking member's CI quiet is active, and no ci_pause key otherwise. (3) Pause file: the member writes it atomically (temp file in the same directory, then rename) as {until, until_unix, set_unix, reason} while ci_pause is present and removes it when absent; its path is the runner unit's NOVA_CI_PAUSE_FILE: linux the seat's $HOME/nova-bench/runners/ci-pause.json, darwin /Users/Shared/nova-runners/ci-pause.json (mode 2775, group staff: the darwin runner seat is a separate account). (4) Gate, per runner, FAIL OPEN: a pause is active iff the file parses, now < until_unix and until_unix <= set_unix + 86400; absent, unreadable, past or over the cap is no pause, CI on, so a dead sprint or member can never leave CI off. States: enabled -> draining (pause active, the runner's Runner.Worker is running a job) -> paused (child stopped by SIGINT) -> enabled (pause not active: child started again); enabled goes straight to paused when idle; draining goes back to enabled with no signal when the pause lifts first. DRAIN, NEVER KILL: the gate never signals a child whose Runner.Worker is running. It polls every NOVA_GATE_EVERY seconds (default 15). (5) Gate report: <pause dir>/state/<runner name>.json {state, at_unix} on each change. (6) Beat and view: the member sends fleet beat <m> --runners enabled=<n>,draining=<n>,paused=<n>,unmanaged=<n> (unmanaged: a Runner.Listener with no state file, e.g. one started from a shell outside its unit) only when the installed fleet beat -h lists --runners; the beat stores the counts beside no_room and refuses a malformed word naming the form; where and where --all show one cell per fleet member: ci=on, ci=paused until <time>, ci=draining <n>, ci=unmanaged <n> (the remedy is a converge of that host's runners), ci=- with no counts; where --json carries the same. Unit tests use no real time (clocks start at 1000 seconds), no sockets, no network: fakes for the store, the server, the process table and the files; real runner control is functional-tier only, and converging the fleet stays the coordinator's act."
   :why "fleet quiet and fleet hold stop sprint deals only; nothing pauses a machine's GitHub Actions runners, and a quiet window on amd-1 on 2026-10-16 was made by stopping its runners by hand. A pause must let running jobs finish, can never stay on past its deadline, and shows in the sprint's views."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "Glenn, live, 2026-10-16: 'Please add a card so you can disable CI from running on machines as needed. 1.7'")
  (item "roadmap-as-feature-tables" :group "docs" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "ROADMAP.md is feature tables with check marks and crosses, like schema's"
   :text "Every repository's roadmap renders as one section per area and one table of user-recognisable features with done and remaining cells and a complete percentage row, evidence for each done cell kept in the data, as schema's ROADMAP.md does. pkg/roadmap (nova-tools) gains feature and evidence records and the table render; nova-work roadmap verbs and nova-sprint pick it up with the next nova-tools bump, so both repos switch together. Locked out of v1.4.8 because the renderer ships inside nova-work and nova-sprint."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "the owner, 2026-10-17: the standard format for all roadmaps, including nova tools and nova sprint")
  (item "land-conflict-is-a-merge-step" :group "sprint" :date "2026-10-16"
   :title "A landing conflict is resolved in the merge step, never a new attempt"
   :text "When a card's head no longer merges onto its stream's tip, the lander merges the tip into the card's branch, re-runs the card's own gate and lands; only a conflict it cannot resolve, or a gate that goes red after the merge, goes back to the card, and then as a merge task, not a fresh attempt with a new worker and a new read. Today every conflict re-deals the card (fixed-count reached attempt 11 on a two-line make/go.mk conflict)."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "the owner, 2026-10-17: these bad merges should not become fixes; they should be done in the merge step")
  (item "v17-one-roadmap-format" :group "docs" :date "2026-10-17"
   :title "One roadmap format for every repository, one renderer, and defer writes it"
   :text "Every repository (nova-sprint, nova-tools, schema, rocketnet, ideas and the rest) keeps its roadmap in one form, and one renderer produces the feature-table ROADMAP.md (generated check marks and crosses, as schema's is; see roadmap-as-feature-tables). nova-sprint defer, and anything else that writes a roadmap, writes that one form. Existing roadmaps are migrated once. Problem, two forms today: nova-sprint keeps docs/roadmap.sexp as `(roadmap \"v1\" ...)` rendered by `go tool roadmap` (make roadmap); mas-bandwidth/schema keeps its roadmap as `(:schema 1 :root ... :nodes (...))` rendered to ROADMAP.md by tools/roadmap/render.lisp. nova-sprint defer writes a deferred card's brief into a checkout's docs/roadmap.sexp, but only in the sprint's own form, so on schema it refused: `does not decode: roadmap: top: want one (roadmap \"v1\" ...) form`, and the schema non-Go streams had to be recorded by hand. Smallest fix: choose the one form with the owner, make the single renderer read it, make defer and every writer emit it, and migrate each existing roadmap once with a check that the rendered ROADMAP.md is unchanged in content. Tools: nova-sprint defer, the roadmap renderer (go tool roadmap), schema tools/roadmap/render.lisp. Tests are unit tests: no real time, no sockets, no network."
   :why "Two roadmap forms mean defer cannot write to schema, and records are made by hand."
   :release "v1.7"
   :origin "the owner, 2026-10-17: 'please log this for v1.7. We should have only one roadmap format.'")
  ; Moved out of the sprint on 2026-10-18: v15-schema-tables, target v1.7
